.TH BOOT-REPAIR 1 "September 2026" "Boot Bitch 0.2.24" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
boot-repair \- graphical Linux recovery and guarded boot-repair utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boot-repair
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Boot Bitch
is a graphical Qt 6 Linux utility for inspecting the running host or repairing
another Linux installation from a separate recovery environment. It provides protected
storage discovery, read-only diagnostics, encrypted-volume handling, Btrfs
snapshot recovery, and a guarded privileged repair backend.
.PP
Ordinary repair-target work requires a different booted Linux environment, such
as a live Linux USB or another Linux installation on a different physical drive.
The running host is protected from ordinary target selection. Explicit Host
Maintenance enables supported native maintenance of the active Debian/Ubuntu-family
system after independent host identity and boot-mount checks.
.SH WORKFLOW
Select and commit a physical repair drive in Systems, unlock its LUKS volume
when necessary, then run Diagnostics for the Repair Target. Run All creates a
complete authoritative read-only report. Review the report before selecting an
individual repair or a Full Repair plan. After any target write, configuration
edit, snapshot rollback, Host-to-Repair copy, or shell command that may have
changed files, rerun the affected diagnostic. Full Repair requires fresh
matching evidence for every selected stage on the same target; Run All is the
simplest way to populate the complete set, while a one-stage plan can use that
stage's fresh diagnostic.
.PP
For native host work, select Host Maintenance on the Running Host card, choose
Running Host in Diagnostics, and run the required fresh diagnostics before
starting a supported repair stage. Snapshot rollback, the Chroot Shell and
repair-side File Copy browsing continue to operate on the separate repair target.
.PP
A disabled repair button means that no target is committed, the target is
locked or unsupported, the required diagnostic is stale or missing, a required
capability is unavailable, or the operation is deliberately opt-in (for
example EFI repair). The Repair page states the condition and the diagnostic to
run. Read-only inspection and a failed command that reports no target change do
not invalidate diagnostic evidence.
.SH REPAIR SUPPORT
Version 0.2.24 supports Debian, Ubuntu, and compatible systems for guarded
package repair, DKMS, adaptive package upgrades, initramfs, GRUB, distribution-
aware EFI/UKI recovery, boot-stack reconciliation, and transactional Btrfs
root rollback on supported layouts. Graphical-login recovery examines the
target default target, display-manager.service, installed packages and units,
and recent boot evidence. It restores an unambiguous configured or last-boot
manager such as SDDM, GDM3, LightDM, greetd, ly, or a custom systemd unit. A
headless preflight verifies the unit and executable; no graphical session is
started inside the repair chroot.
.PP
Boot evidence describes the detected chain. TUXEDO Debian-base systems with
the vendor UKI builder use the TUXEDO UKI before initramfs and the root LUKS
unlock; TUXEDO Ubuntu layouts without that builder use the GRUB menu first.
EFI records are classified as UKI, fallback, WebFAI/iPXE, vendor loader, or
device-path fallback, and maintained BootOrder groups each drive's routes
without retaining duplicate destinations.
.PP
Diagnostics are read-only and cached per system scope and diagnostic. Re-running a
diagnostic replaces its cache; Run All replaces the complete set. Target
writes invalidate related evidence and are marked stale in Logs. Every
modifying operation requires explicit confirmation. The first privileged action
opens one whitelisted administrator helper session through Polkit; later
privileged actions reuse it until the user locks the session or exits. LUKS
passphrases are sent over standard input and are never logged.
.SH SAFETY
For ordinary repair requests, the helper repeats the host-protection check and
refuses a target that resolves to a disk backing /, /boot, or /boot/efi on the
running system. Host Maintenance separately validates the active host identity,
writable boot mounts and package locks and guards firmware variables from
indirect package or vendor hooks. Temporary target
mounts are removed after each operation. Existing LUKS mappings are reused;
mappings opened by Boot Bitch are closed when its administrator session ends.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/BootRepair/
Per-user Qt settings.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/boot-repair/boot-repair-helper
Whitelisted privileged repair backend installed by the Debian package.
.TP
.I /var/log/boot-repair-session.log
Most recent target-side privileged repair session log, when writable.
